  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At constant temperature, V ∝ 1/P, so P × V is constant.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Charles' law relates volume and absolute temperature at constant pressure.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    P₁V₁ = P₂V₂: 1 × 200 = 2 × V₂, so V₂ = 100 cm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V = 0.5 × 22.4 = 11.2 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At room temperature and pressure (298 K) one mole occupies 24 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each gas contributes its own pressure independently, and the total is their sum.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    P(gas) = P(total) − P(water vapour).
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rate ∝ 1/√M, so lighter gases diffuse faster.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen has the smallest molar mass, so it diffuses fastest.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Random motion with elastic collisions is a basic assumption of the kinetic model.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Pressure is the force per unit area produced by countless collisions with the walls.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Kinetic energy depends only on the kelvin temperature, not on the identity of the gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    0 K is the temperature at which particle motion would stop.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    48 ÷ 2 = 24 dm³ per mole, which is the value at r.t.p.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    By the pressure law P ∝ T at constant volume, so doubling P doubles T in kelvin.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V ∝ T, and 600 ÷ 300 = 2, so the volume doubles.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At low pressure and high temperature the particles are far apart and attractions are negligible.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ when pressure is in pascals and volume in m³.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At high pressure the particles are so close that their own volume and their attractions matter.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    r(O₂)/r(H₂) = √(2/32) = 1/4, so oxygen diffuses four times more slowly.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V₂ = 300 × (750/760) × (273/300) ≈ 269 cm³: cooling and a small pressure rise both shrink it.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Boyle's law: P and V are inversely proportional.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Avogadro's law: equal volumes contain equal numbers of molecules, so one mole occupies the same volume.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    P ∝ T at constant V, so P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V = 0.25 × 22.4 = 5.6 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Heating raises the pressure because the volume is fixed — this is the pressure law.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Temperature measures mean kinetic energy, so at the same temperature both are equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂ = (1 × 250) ÷ 100 = 2.5 atm.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Faster particles collide harder, so at constant pressure the gas expands.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V = 3 × 22.4 = 67.2 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V ∝ T, so halving the kelvin temperature halves the volume.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Density is proportional to molar mass, and CO₂ has the largest M.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1520 ÷ 760 = 2 atm.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At constant temperature the product of pressure and volume is constant.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ideal model assumes negligible forces between particles.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At constant pressure the volume doubles with the kelvin temperature.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rate ∝ 1/√M, so the lighter gas diffuses faster.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dalton's law: the partial pressures add.
  ]

